  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 229 PM MDT TUE OCT 20 2026\nCool and wet conditions will continue as a broad low pressure\nsystem moves northeast on Friday. Breezy northwest winds with \ngusts ranging from 30-45 mph, widespread rain over southwest \nIdaho, and cool temperatures about 5-8 degrees below normal are \nanticipated during the day on Friday. Snow levels will lower\nby Friday afternoon, down to around 6000-7000 ft MSL. Higher \nelevation passes and ridgetops will likely only see a dusting\nof snow, with summits seeing close to an inch as the precipitation\ntapers off early Saturday. Drying conditions and near normal \ntemperatures will return for the weekend thanks to a building \nridge of high pressure over the West Coast. By late Sunday, long\nrange forecast models begin to diverge. Some ensemble members \nfavor a closed low pressure system moving onshore and bringing \nmuch cooler and wetter conditions by early next week, while \nother deterministic runs favor high pressure lingering over the \nIntermountain West through the middle of next week. Current \nforecast favors ridging and a slight warming and drying trend \nthrough Monday, with a slight chance of precipitation over the \nWest Central Mountains.",
